"# What is Spark's Architecture?\n\nApache Spark follows a distributed master-worker architecture, where a central Driver coordinates the execution of tasks across multiple Executors running on worker nodes.\n\nSpark uses a Cluster Manager to allocate computing resources and a DAG-based execution engine to divide computations into jobs, stages, and tasks.\n\nA Spark application consists of a Driver process and one or more Executor processes that work together to process data in parallel.\n\n## 1. Spark Architecture Diagram\n\nConceptual architecture. The Cluster Manager allocates resources, while the Driver communicates directly with Executors to schedule tasks. Real clusters may contain many workers and multiple executors per worker.\n\n## 2. Main Components of Spark Architecture\n\n| Component       | Description                                                                  |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Driver          | Central coordinator that manages the Spark application and schedules work    |\n| SparkSession    | Main entry point for DataFrame and SQL operations                            |\n| SparkContext    | Core interface connecting the application with Spark's execution environment |\n| Cluster Manager | Allocates resources for running the application                              |\n| Worker Node     | Machine providing CPU and memory resources                                   |\n| Executor        | Process that executes tasks and stores cached or intermediate data           |\n| Task            | Smallest schedulable unit of work executed by an executor                    |\n\n## 3. How Spark Architecture Works — Execution Flow\n\nConsider this PySpark application:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"s3://bucket/sales/\")\n\nresult = (\n    df.filter(\"amount > 1000\")\n      .groupBy(\"region\")\n      .count()\n)\n\nresult.show()\n```\n\nHere is how it executes at a high level.\n\n[spark-reference:architecture-execution]\n\n## 4. Spark's Logical Execution Architecture\n\nApart from the physical components, Spark also has a logical execution hierarchy.\n\nIllustrative hierarchy: the stages form a dependency graph, so not all stages necessarily run simultaneously.\n\n- Application: A complete Spark program containing the Driver and its Executors.\n- Job: A computation triggered by an action such as `count()`, `collect()`, or a write. An action can sometimes lead to more than one Spark job.\n- Stage: A set of tasks that can execute without requiring a shuffle boundary between them. Stages have dependencies on other stages.\n- Task: An individual unit of execution, typically processing one partition within a stage.\n\nSpark uses its DAG Scheduler and Task Scheduler to coordinate this execution.\n\n## 5. Client Mode vs Cluster Mode\n\nSpark supports two important deployment modes.\n\n| Client Mode                                             | Cluster Mode                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Driver runs in the submitting client environment        | Driver runs inside the cluster                                                 |\n| Useful for interactive development and debugging        | Common for scheduled production applications                                   |\n| Client must remain connected while the application runs | Submitting client generally does not need to remain connected after submission |\n\nIn both modes, Executors run on cluster resources, and the overall execution architecture remains conceptually the same.\n\n## Interview-ready answer (60–90 seconds)\n\n> Apache Spark follows a distributed master-worker architecture consisting mainly of a Driver, Cluster Manager, Worker Nodes, and Executors.\n>\n> The Driver is the central coordinator of the Spark application. It initializes SparkSession and SparkContext and manages the scheduling and execution of jobs.\n>\n> The Cluster Manager, which can be YARN, Kubernetes, or Spark Standalone, allocates computing resources. Executors are launched on worker nodes to perform distributed processing.\n>\n> When a Spark action is triggered, Spark creates an execution plan and organizes the computation into jobs, stages, and tasks. Stages are determined by execution dependencies, including shuffle boundaries, and each stage contains tasks that process individual data partitions.\n>\n> The Driver schedules these tasks on Executors, which execute them in parallel and communicate execution status back to the Driver. The processed results are then returned to the Driver or written to external storage.\n>\n> Therefore, Spark architecture separates resource management, execution coordination, and distributed data processing, allowing applications to scale across multiple machines.\n\nKey point to remember: Spark architecture has two important perspectives: physical architecture (Driver → Cluster Manager → Executors) and logical execution architecture (Application → Jobs → Stages → Tasks)."
